// Cs10.grammar — C# 10.0: file-scoped namespace (T3.10.1), global using directive (T3.10.2).
//
// A file-scoped namespace uses a single ";" instead of a "{ ... }" block:
//   namespace MyNamespace;
//
//   class MyClass { }
// vs the block-scoped form (Cs1.grammar:17):
//   namespace MyNamespace
//   {
//       class MyClass { }
//   }
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src\Compilers\CSharp\Portable):
//   * ParseNamespaceDeclarationCore (Parser/LanguageParser.cs:247-328): after the "namespace"
//     keyword + ParseQualifiedName(), the parser branches on the next token:
//       - SemicolonToken (264-267): eat ";" -> FILE-SCOPED namespace;
//       - OpenBraceToken / possible member (268-273): eat "{" -> BLOCK-scoped namespace.
//     The file-scoped path calls ParseNamespaceBody (292) with parentKind
//     FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration and builds FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration
//     (attributeLists, modifiers, namespaceToken, name, semicolon, body.Externs, body.Usings,
//     body.Members) (295-303).
//   * FileScopedNamespaceDeclarationSyntax (Syntax.xml.Syntax.Generated.cs:9789): NamespaceKeyword
//     (9813) + Name (9815) + SemicolonToken (9817) + Externs/Usings/Members (9819-9823). The body
//     (externs, usings, members) is the SAME member set as a block-scoped namespace body.
//   * SyntaxKind.FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration (Syntax/SyntaxKind.cs:800); IsNamespaceDeclaration
//     (SyntaxKindFacts.cs:399).
//   * Version gate — a BINDER/semantic (C# 10.0) feature; the PARSER accepts the form wherever the
//     rule exists. Version purity comes from the rule being Cs10-only.
//
// Model: a NEW NamespaceMember alternative (T0.3 append-merge) — a file-scoped namespace is a
// member (like a block-scoped NamespaceDeclaration, Cs1.grammar:17) whose body is the SAME
// NamespaceBody (Cs1.grammar:19, NamespaceMember*) a block-scoped namespace uses. The greedy
// NamespaceBody* consumes the rest of the file (Roslyn: the file-scoped body runs to end-of-file,
// 292), so all subsequent using/extern/namespace/type members belong to it.
//
// Disambiguation FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration vs NamespaceDeclaration (longest-match, mutually
// exclusive): both start "namespace" + QualifiedName; the token after the name disambiguates —
// "{" -> block-scoped (NamespaceDeclaration requires the literal "{", Cs1.grammar:17), ";" ->
// file-scoped. A token cannot be both, so no equal-length tie.
//
// CS10 only: at v9 the FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration alternative is ABSENT, so "namespace a;"
// matches no NamespaceMember (the block-scoped NamespaceDeclaration needs "{") -> unconsumed ->
// REJECTS (see Cs1RoslynDirectiveTests.Namespace_FileScopedSemicolon_Fails, v1).
//
// Boundary (parser-vs-binder split): a SECOND file-scoped namespace after the first ("namespace A;
// namespace B;") is accepted by the parser (nested in A's body) but is a BINDER error; consistent
// with the plan's parser/binder separation.

// T3.10.2 — C# 10.0: the `global using` directive.
//
// A `global using` is a using directive with a `global` prefix that applies to the entire assembly:
//   global using System;
//   global using static System.Console;
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src\Compilers\CSharp\Portable):
//   * ParseUsingDirective (Parser/LanguageParser.cs:942-1010): the FIRST token is an OPTIONAL `global`
//     (949-951: `CurrentToken.ContextualKind == GlobalKeyword ? EatToken() : null`), then the REQUIRED
//     `using` (955), an OPTIONAL `static` (956, `TryEatToken(StaticKeyword)`), an optional `unsafe`,
//     an optional alias (`IsNamedAssignment() ? ParseNameEquals() : null`, 967), the name
//     (`alias == null ? ParseQualifiedName() : ParseType()`, 1000), and the `;` (1006).
//   * UsingDirectiveSyntax (Syntax/UsingDirectiveSyntax.cs:21-22, Syntax.xml.Syntax.Generated.cs:9518):
//     `GlobalKeyword UsingKeyword StaticKeyword? UnsafeKeyword? Alias? NamespaceOrType SemicolonToken`.
//   * Version gate — a BINDER/semantic (C# 10.0) feature: `MessageID.IDS_FeatureGlobalUsing` is checked
//     in Declarations/DeclarationTreeBuilder.cs:484-485 when `usingDirective.GlobalKeyword` is non-default.
//     The PARSER accepts the form wherever the rule exists; version purity comes from the rule being
//     Cs10-only.
//   * SyntaxKind.GlobalKeyword (Syntax/SyntaxKind.cs:341).
//
// Model: a NEW NamespaceMember alternative (T0.3 append-merge). It mirrors the existing UsingDirective
// (Cs1.grammar:11-13) but with a leading `"global"`, and ADDS the `static` form (the non-global
// UsingDirective has no `static` form — a pre-existing gap; Roslyn's UsingDirectiveSyntax has `StaticKeyword?`).
//
// Disambiguation (longest-match, mutually exclusive on the token right after `using`):
//   - a TypeName followed by `=`  -> GlobalAliasUsingDirective;
//   - the reserved `static`       -> GlobalStaticUsingDirective;
//   - a QualifiedName followed by `;` -> GlobalOpenUsingDirective.
// `static` is a reserved keyword (Cs1.grammar:619), so `TypeName`/`QualifiedName` (TypeName =
// !ReservedKeyword Identifier, Cs1.grammar:23) cannot consume it: on `global using static ...` the
// alias/open alternatives FAIL and only the static alternative matches -> no equal-length tie.
// vs the Cs1 UsingDirective (Cs1.grammar:11): that starts with `"using"`, this starts with `"global"`
// -> different first token, so a `global`-prefixed directive only ever matches GlobalUsingDirective.
//
// CS10 only: at v9 the GlobalUsingDirective alternative is ABSENT. `global` is not a reserved keyword
// (absent from Cs1.grammar:556-637) but it is a bare Identifier that no NamespaceMember alternative
// consumes (each starts with a reserved keyword or `[`) -> `NamespaceMember*` matches zero members,
// `global using ...` is unconsumed -> REJECTS (see Cs10GlobalUsingTests.*_RejectedAtV9).
//
// Boundary (out of scope, matches the non-global UsingDirective): the `unsafe` keyword
// (`global using unsafe static ...`) is not modeled; Roslyn's UsingDirectiveSyntax has `UnsafeKeyword?`.

// === Namespace member union: APPEND the file-scoped namespace + the global using directive (T0.3 merge). ===
NamespaceMember =
    | FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration
    | GlobalUsingDirective;

// File-scoped namespace (C# 10.0): "namespace" QualifiedName ";" NamespaceBody?.
// The body is the same NamespaceBody (Cs1.grammar:19) as a block-scoped namespace. It is wrapped in
// Optional ("?") exactly like the block-scoped NamespaceDeclaration (Cs1.grammar:17): the engine's
// zero-or-many reports a Failure when its element cannot match at the position (e.g. EOF for an
// empty body), and the Optional swallows that Failure (NoneNode) so "namespace A;" alone is valid.
FileScopedNamespaceDeclaration = "namespace" QualifiedName ";" NamespaceBody?;

// Global using directive (C# 10.0): "global" "using" [static] [alias =] name ";".
// Mirrors the Cs1 UsingDirective (Cs1.grammar:11-13) with a leading "global" and an added `static` form.
GlobalUsingDirective =
    | GlobalAliasUsingDirective = "global" "using" TypeName "=" QualifiedName ";"
    | GlobalStaticUsingDirective = "global" "using" "static" QualifiedName ";"
    | GlobalOpenUsingDirective = "global" "using" QualifiedName ";";